    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\nPaul plants ten snowdrop bulbs.\n\nHe knows from experience that the bulbs have a $65\\%$ chance of flowering in the spring.\n\nFind the probability that:\n\n(i) exactly 7 snowdrops flower in the spring; \\hfill [3]\n\n(ii) at least 7 snowdrops flower in the spring; \\hfill [4]\n\n(iii) at most 7 snowdrops flower in the spring. \\hfill [3]\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2BinomialDistributionProbabilityQ001TikZ-001]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\nLet $X$ be the random variable \"number of snowdrops flowering in spring\".\n\n$$\nX\\sim B(10,0.65)\n$$\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | $P(X=7)={}^{10}C_7(0.65)^7(0.35)^3$ | M1, MW1 | 2 | Uses the binomial formula with $n=10$, $p=0.65$ and $x=7$. |\n| 2 | $P(X=7)=0.2522\\ldots=0.252\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Final probability to 3 significant figures. |\n| 3 | $P(X\\ge 7)=P(X=7)+P(X=8)+P(X=9)+P(X=10)$ | M1 | 1 | Uses the correct upper-tail expansion. |\n| 4 | $P(X=8)={}^{10}C_8(0.65)^8(0.35)^2=0.1756\\ldots$ | W1 | 1 | Calculates one of the required upper-tail terms. |\n| 5 | $P(X=9)={}^{10}C_9(0.65)^9(0.35)=0.07249\\ldots$ and $P(X=10)=(0.65)^{10}=0.01346\\ldots$ | W1 | 1 | Calculates the remaining upper-tail terms. |\n| 6 | $P(X\\ge7)=0.252\\ldots+0.1756\\ldots+0.07249\\ldots+0.0134\\ldots=0.513827\\ldots=0.514\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Correctly sums the upper-tail probabilities. |\n| 7 | $P(X\\le7)=1-[P(8)+P(9)+P(10)]$ | M2 | 2 | Uses the complement of $X=8,9,10$. |\n| 8 | $P(X\\le7)=0.73839\\ldots=0.738\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Final lower-tail probability. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n(i) $0.252$  \n(ii) $0.514$  \n(iii) $0.738$\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nThe method is to model the number of flowering bulbs as $X\\sim B(10,0.65)$, then apply exact and cumulative binomial probabilities. Key marks come from using the correct binomial formula, selecting the correct tail, using the complement efficiently where suitable, and giving answers to 3 significant figures.\n\n> Uses the diagram shown above: AS2BinomialDistributionProbabilityQ001TikZ-001"
