    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\n(i) State 2 of the conditions necessary for a random variable to be modelled by a binomial distribution. \\hfill [2]\n\nThe probability that a boy chosen at random has a birthday on a Saturday this year is $\\frac{1}{7}$.\n\n8 boys are chosen at random.\n\n(ii) Find the probability that none of these boys has a birthday on a Saturday this year. \\hfill [4]\n\n(iii) Find the probability that at most 2 of these boys have a birthday on a Saturday this year. \\hfill [4]\n\n(iv) Find the mean and variance of the number of boys with a birthday on a Saturday this year. \\hfill [2]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | Any two: only 2 outcomes to each trial; fixed number of trials; probability of success same for each trial; trials independent. | MW2 | 2 | States two binomial conditions. |\n| 2 | $n=8,\\quad p=\\frac{1}{7},\\quad q=\\frac{6}{7}$ | M1, W2 | 3 | Identifies the number of trials and success/failure probabilities. |\n| 3 | $P(0)=\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)^8$ | M1, W2 | 3 | Uses the probability that none have a Saturday birthday. |\n| 4 | $P(0)=0.291\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Final probability to 3 significant figures. |\n| 5 | $P(0)+P(1)+P(2)$ | MW1 | 1 | Interprets “at most 2” correctly. |\n| 6 | $0.291+{}^{8}C_1\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)^7+{}^{8}C_2\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^2\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)^6$ | MW2 | 2 | Calculates the cumulative probability up to 2. |\n| 7 | $0.291+0.38847+0.226611=0.906\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Final cumulative probability. |\n| 8 | $E(X)=np=8\\times\\frac{1}{7}=1.14\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | MW1 | 1 | Uses the binomial mean formula. |\n| 9 | $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=npq=8\\times\\frac{1}{7}\\times\\frac{6}{7}=0.980\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | MW1 | 1 | Uses the binomial variance formula. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n(i) Any two of: two outcomes, fixed number of trials, constant probability of success, independent trials.  \n(ii) $0.291$  \n(iii) $0.906$  \n(iv) Mean $=1.14$, variance $=0.980$\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nThis question checks both the conditions for a binomial distribution and the mechanics of calculating binomial probabilities. Part (iii) requires “at most 2”, so $0,1,2$ must all be included.\n\n### Note on Mark Scheme Interpretation\n\nThe marks shown beside part (ii) are spatially compressed in the source PDF. The transcription preserves the visible codes and the total mark allocation, but the exact split between the setup line and $P(0)$ line is visually compressed."
