    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\nLast year Pauline visited her local newsagent on 219 out of the 365 days in the year.\n\nAssuming that her visits were at random, find the probability that during a particular week Pauline visited the newsagent on:\n\n(i) exactly five days; \\hfill [3]\n\n(ii) at most two days; \\hfill [3]\n\n(iii) at least two days. \\hfill [3]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\nLet $X$ be the random variable \"the number of times Pauline visits the newsagent during a week\".\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | $X\\sim B\\left(7,\\frac{219}{365}\\right)$ | M1 | 1 | Uses 7 days in the week and the yearly visit probability. |\n| 2 | $X\\sim B\\left(7,\\frac{3}{5}\\right)$ | M1 | 1 | Simplifies $\\frac{219}{365}$ to $\\frac{3}{5}$. |\n| 3 | $P(X=5)={}^{7}C_5\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^5\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)^2=0.26127=0.261\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Final exact-five-days probability. |\n| 4 | $P(X\\le2)=P(0)+P(1)+P(2)$ | MW1 | 1 | Uses the correct lower-tail expansion. |\n| 5 | $P(X\\le2)=(0.4)^7+{}^{7}C_1(0.6)(0.4)^6+{}^{7}C_2(0.6)^2(0.4)^5$ | MW1 | 1 | Substitutes terms for 0, 1 and 2 visits. |\n| 6 | $P(X\\le2)=0.0016384+0.0172032+0.0774144=0.0963\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Final at-most-two probability. |\n| 7 | $P(X\\ge2)=1-[P(0)+P(1)]$ | M1, MW1 | 2 | Uses complement for at least 2 visits. |\n| 8 | $P(X\\ge2)=0.98115=0.981\\text{ (3 s.f.)}$ | W1 | 1 | Final at-least-two probability. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n(i) $0.261$  \n(ii) $0.0963$  \n(iii) $0.981$\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nThe yearly rate must be converted into a daily visit probability, then used in a 7-day binomial model. The common trap is confusing “at most two” with “at least two”."
