    "type": "Exam-Style",
    "marks": 9,
    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\nFig. 1 below shows four forces acting along the sides of a parallelogram ABCD.\n\n(i) Find the magnitude of the resultant of these forces. [7]\n\n(ii) Find the angle this resultant makes with the side AD. [2]\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ005TikZ-001]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "> See solution visual below: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ005TikZ-002\n\n### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | Resolve vertically: $6\\sin40^\\circ+5\\sin40^\\circ=11\\sin40^\\circ=7.07066$. | M2 W1 | 3 | Vertical component. |\n| 2 | Resolve horizontally: $10-8+6\\cos40^\\circ+5\\cos40^\\circ=10.4264$. | M1 W1 | 2 | Horizontal component. |\n| 3 | $R^2=7.07066^2+10.4264^2=158.705$. | M1 | 1 | Magnitude from components. |\n| 4 | $R=12.6\\,\\mathrm{N}$ to 3 s.f. | W1 | 1 | Resultant magnitude. |\n| 5 | $\\tan\\theta=7.07066/10.4264$. | M1 | 1 | Direction. |\n| 6 | $\\theta=34.1^\\circ$ to 3 s.f. | W1 | 1 | Angle to AD. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n`R=12.6 N, angle=34.1°`\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nResolve all forces into components parallel and perpendicular to AD, then recombine.\n\n### Alternative Method\n\nOfficial alternative uses a force triangle with a resultant of $11\\,\\mathrm{N}$ and a $2\\,\\mathrm{N}$ horizontal resultant.\n\n\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ005TikZ-002]\n\n---"
