    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\nFig. 2 below shows a crate being pulled along a rough horizontal surface by a light inextensible rope inclined at $30^\\circ$ to the horizontal.\n\nThe crate has a mass of $5\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ and the tension in the rope is $T$ newtons. The coefficient of friction between the crate and the surface is $\\mu$.\n\n(i) Draw a diagram showing the external forces acting on the crate. [2]\n\nWhen $T=15\\,\\mathrm{N}$, the crate is in limiting equilibrium.\n\n(ii) Find $\\mu$. [6]\n\n$T$ is now increased so that the crate accelerates along the surface at $2\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$.\n\n(iii) Find the new value of $T$. [5]\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ013TikZ-001]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "> See solution visual below: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ013TikZ-002\n\n### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | Force diagram with reaction $R$, friction, weight $5g$, and tension at $30^\\circ$. | MW2 | 2 | Correct forces. |\n| 2 | $R+15\\sin30^\\circ=5g$, so $R=41.5$. | M1 M1 W1 | 3 | Vertical equilibrium. |\n| 3 | $F_r=15\\cos30^\\circ=12.99$. | MW1 | 1 | Horizontal limiting equilibrium. |\n| 4 | $F_r=\\mu R$, so $\\mu=12.99/41.5=0.313$. | M1 MW1 | 2 | Coefficient of friction. |\n| 5 | New tension: $R=5g-T\\sin30^\\circ$. | MW1 | 1 | Updated normal reaction. |\n| 6 | $T\\cos30^\\circ-0.313(5g-T\\sin30^\\circ)=5(2)$. | M1 MW2 | 3 | Newton’s second law horizontally. |\n| 7 | $T=24.8\\,\\mathrm{N}$. | W1 | 1 | New tension. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n`mu=0.313; T=24.8 N`\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nThe angled pull reduces the normal reaction, so the friction changes with T.\n\n\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ013TikZ-002]\n\n---"
