    "type": "Exam-Style",
    "marks": 9,
    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\nA small bead, B, of mass $0.3\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ is threaded onto a fixed, rough, horizontal rod. The bead is pulled along the rod by a light inextensible string which makes an angle of $40^\\circ$ with the horizontal. The tension in the string is $T$ newtons. The coefficient of friction between the bead and the rod is $\\mu$. The string and rod are in the same vertical plane.\n\n(i) Complete the diagram below to show the external forces acting on the bead. [2]\n\nWhen $T=1.2\\,\\mathrm{N}$, the bead moves along the rod at a constant speed.\n\n(ii) Find $\\mu$. [7]\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ019TikZ-001]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "> See solution visual below: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ019TikZ-002\n\n### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | Force diagram with reaction $R$, tension $T$, friction, and weight $0.3g$. | MW2 | 2 | Correct forces. |\n| 2 | $R+T\\sin40^\\circ=0.3g$. | M1 W1 | 2 | Vertical equilibrium. |\n| 3 | $R+1.2\\sin40^\\circ=0.3g$, so $R=2.16865$. |  |  | Substitution. |\n| 4 | $F_r=T\\cos40^\\circ=1.2\\cos40^\\circ=0.91925$. | M2 W1 | 3 | Horizontal constant-speed equilibrium. |\n| 5 | $F_r=\\mu R$. | M1 | 1 | Friction law. |\n| 6 | $\\mu=F_r/R=0.42388=0.424$. | W1 | 1 | Coefficient. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n`mu=0.424`\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nThe upward component of the string reduces the normal reaction, which then changes the friction.\n\n\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2ForcesNewtonsLawsQ019TikZ-002]\n\n---"
