    "type": "Exam-Style",
    "marks": 11,
    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\nFig. 3 below shows a skier of mass $78\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ being pulled up a smooth slope by a rope. The slope is inclined at an angle of $20^\\circ$ to the horizontal. The rope is inclined at an angle of $60^\\circ$ to the slope.\n\nModel the skier as a particle.\n\n(i) Draw a diagram showing all the external forces acting on the skier. [2]\n\nThe skier is moving at a constant speed of $2\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\n(ii) Find the tension in the rope. [4]\n\nWhen the skier is $26\\,\\mathrm{m}$ from the bottom of the slope, she lets go of the rope.\n\n(iii) Find the speed of the skier when she reaches the bottom of the slope. [5]\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2InclinedPlanesQ002TikZ-001]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "> See solution visual below: AS2InclinedPlanesQ002TikZ-002\n\n### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\nOfficial solution not found in supplied mark scheme PDF.\n\n> **Note:** Official mark scheme unavailable. Generated solution shown for study support.\n\n### Generated solution, not from official mark scheme\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | Along the slope at constant speed: $T\\cos60^\\circ-78g\\sin20^\\circ=0$. |  |  | Resolve along the smooth slope. |\n| 2 | $T=2(78g\\sin20^\\circ)=523\\,\\mathrm{N}$ to 3 s.f. |  |  | Tension in rope. |\n| 3 | After letting go, acceleration down the slope has magnitude $g\\sin20^\\circ$. Take up the slope as positive: $a=-g\\sin20^\\circ$, $u=2$, $s=-26$. |  |  | Motion after release. |\n| 4 | $v^2=2^2+2(-g\\sin20^\\circ)(-26)$. |  |  | SUVAT. |\n| 5 | $v=13.3\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ to 3 s.f. |  |  | Speed at bottom. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n`T≈523 N; speed≈13.3 m s^{-1}`\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nGenerated solution: resolve the tension component along the slope for the constant-speed part, then use SUVAT after the rope is released.\n\n\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2InclinedPlanesQ002TikZ-002]\n\n---"
