    "type": "Exam-Style",
    "marks": 9,
    "questionMarkdown": "### Question\n\nFig. 2 below shows a car of mass $1300\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ ascending a hill inclined at an angle of $20^\\circ$ to the horizontal.\n\nThe coefficient of friction between the car and the hill is $0.25$. The tractive force produced by the engine of the car is $12000\\,\\mathrm{N}$.\n\n(i) Draw a diagram showing all the external forces acting on the car. [2]\n\n(ii) Find the acceleration of the car. [7]\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2InclinedPlanesQ005TikZ-001]",
    "markSchemeMarkdown": "> See solution visual below: AS2InclinedPlanesQ005TikZ-002\n\n### Official Mark Scheme Solution\n\n| Step | Official working / result | Mark code | Mark awarded | Brief annotation |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---|\n| 1 | Force diagram with $N$, tractive force $12000$, friction, acceleration and $1300g$. | MW2 | 2 | Correct forces. |\n| 2 | $N-1300g\\cos20^\\circ=0$, so $N=11971.68$. | M1 W1 | 2 | Normal reaction. |\n| 3 | $F_r=\\mu N=11971.68(0.25)=2992.92$. | M1 W1 | 2 | Friction. |\n| 4 | $12000-2992.92-1300g\\cos70^\\circ=1300a$. | M1 W1 | 2 | Resolve up slope. |\n| 5 | $4649.76=1300a$, so $a=3.58\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ to 3 s.f. | W1 | 1 | Acceleration. |\n\n### Final Answer\n\n`a=3.58 m s^{-2}`\n\n### What the Examiner Wanted\n\nThe component of weight down a $20^\\circ$ slope can be written as $1300g\\sin20^\\circ$ or $1300g\\cos70^\\circ$.\n\n\n\n[VISUAL REFERENCE: AS2InclinedPlanesQ005TikZ-002]\n\n---"
